[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=PrivacyFence companion
Comment=Opens connector sign-in pages for the privilege-separated PrivacyFence daemon
Exec=__COMPANION_EXECUTABLE__ --serve
Icon=privacyfence
Terminal=false
Categories=Utility;
NoDisplay=true
X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=true
# #428 Phase 4 (B5b): the other half of the startup inversion ADR 0002
# describes, and the Linux counterpart of the macOS companion LaunchAgent.
#
# Before Phase 4, what autostarted in the user's session was the daemon
# (/etc/xdg/autostart/privacyfence.desktop, which
# scripts/linux_privilege_separation.sh moves aside). Afterwards the daemon
# is a system unit with no session at all, and what autostarts here is
# `privacyfence-companion --serve`: the companion's control channel and
# nothing else, so the daemon's connector OAuth flows (oauth_loopback.py)
# have something in the user's session to hand a browser URL to. Without it,
# a separated daemon's `webbrowser.open()` has no display to open anything
# on -- the same Session 0 problem ADR 0002 decision 5 anticipates for
# Windows, which arrives on Linux the moment the daemon stops sharing the
# human's session.
#
# NoDisplay=true, unlike the companion's *other* .desktop file
# (resources/linux/privacyfence-companion.desktop, installed to
# /usr/share/applications/): that one is the clickable Applications-menu
# entry with Open Approvals / Settings / Quit, this one is autostart-only
# and has no menu presence of its own. One file cannot serve both roles --
# a menu click wants a one-shot --action, autostart wants a process that
# stays up -- which is the same reason the daemon's autostart entry and the
# companion's menu entry are already two separate files.
#
# scripts/linux_privilege_separation.sh renders this to
# /etc/xdg/autostart/privacyfence-companion.desktop (machine-wide, so every
# user who logs in gets one, and the user who ran the installer is
# additionally in the service group that lets it actually connect).
